{"graph_chars": "Labor supply slopes up because of a tradeoff: work versus leisure. A higher wage, and more people give up leisure. Table four point three. Number of workers: immigration, population, demographics — more women working outside the home — shift supply right. Required education cuts supply. Fewer Ph.D. mathematicians than high-school math teachers. Fewer cardiologists than primary-care doctors. Fewer physicians than nurses. Government policy: tougher licenses cut supply. Subsidies to nursing schools shift nurse supply right. Child-care benefits can raise mothers' labor supply. Long unemployment benefits can slow job search. A change in the salary moves you along the curves. Events on that list shift them, and the market finds a new wage and a new quantity.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0624], [0.0624, 0.1249], [0.1249, 0.1873], [0.1873, 0.2497], [0.2497, 0.3121], [0.3121, 0.3746], [0.3746, 0.437], [0.437, 0.4994], [0.4994, 0.5619], [0.5619, 0.6243], [0.6243, 0.6867], [0.6867, 0.7491], [0.7491, 0.8116], [0.8116, 0.874], [0.874, 0.9364], [0.9364, 0.9988], [0.9988, 1.0613], [1.0613, 1.1237], [1.1237, 1.1861], [1.1861, 1.2486], [1.2486, 1.311], [1.311, 1.3734], [1.3734, 1.4358], [1.4358, 1.4983], [1.4983, 1.5607], [1.5607, 1.6231], [1.6231, 1.6856], [1.6856, 1.748], [1.748, 1.8104], [1.8104, 1.8728], [1.8728, 1.9353], [1.9353, 1.9977], [1.9977, 2.0601], [2.0601, 2.1225], [2.1225, 2.185], [2.185, 2.2474], [2.2474, 2.3098], [2.3098, 2.3723], [2.3723, 2.4347], [2.4347, 2.4971], [2.4971, 2.5595], [2.5595, 2.622], [2.622, 2.6844], [2.6844, 2.7468], [2.7468, 2.8093], [2.8093, 2.8717], [2.8717, 2.9341], [2.9341, 2.9965], [2.9965, 3.059], [3.059, 3.1214], [3.1214, 3.1838], [3.1838, 3.2462], [3.2462, 3.3087], [3.3087, 3.3711], [3.3711, 3.4335], [3.4335, 3.496], [3.496, 3.5584], [3.5584, 3.6208], [3.6208, 3.6832], [3.6832, 3.7457], [3.7457, 3.8081], [3.8081, 3.8705], [3.8705, 3.933], [3.933, 3.9954], [3.9954, 4.0578], [4.0578, 4.1202], [4.1202, 4.1827], [4.1827, 4.2451],